      "title": "Iran has right to peaceful use of nuclear energy - Iranian FM spokesman",
      "description": "Iran has right to peaceful use of nuclear energy - Iranian FM spokesman melissa_fernan\u2026 Mon, 16 Nov 2026 - 12:52 IRAN has the right to use nuclear energy for peaceful purposes as a state party to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (NPT), Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei said at a press conference on Sunday. Rejecting Germany's demand to halt uranium enrichment on Sunday, Baghaei reiterated Iran's right under the NPT to the peaceful use of nuclear energy. Baghaei said Iran's right to enrich uranium was not granted by Germany, France or any other country. As an NPT party, Iran would continue to fulfill its obligations, he said. Iran did not suspend the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA)'s safeguards monitoring of relevant facilities during its conflict with the United States and Israel, said Baghaei, adding that most of Iran's cooperation with the agency had been interrupted because of circumstances imposed on the country. Baghaei said Iran would not relinquish its right to peaceful nuclear energy, and it is not currently discussing the details of the nuclear issue with the United States, and cooperation between Iran and the IAEA will remain unchanged under current circumstances. Whether Iran withdraws from the NPT should be decided by the relevant Iranian authorities in accordance with legal procedures, he said. Disable Ads Off Brand Safe On IRAN has the right to use nuclear energy for peaceful purposes as a state party to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (NPT), Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei said at a press conference on Sunday. Rejecting Germany's demand to halt uranium enrichment on Sunday, Baghaei reiterated Iran's right under the NPT to the peaceful use of nuclear energy. Baghaei said Iran's right to enrich uranium was not granted by Germany, France or any other country. As an NPT party, Iran would continue to fulfill its obligations, he said. Iran d